1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,635
Look under almost any railway track in

2
00:00:02,635 --> 00:00:04,716
the world and you will see the

3
00:00:04,716 --> 00:00:05,479
same thing.

4
00:00:05,659 --> 00:00:06,356
Rocks.

5
00:00:06,536 --> 00:00:08,148
Millions of sharp,

6
00:00:08,148 --> 00:00:11,641
gray stones, piled under and around the

7
00:00:11,641 --> 00:00:12,178
rails.

8
00:00:12,358 --> 00:00:15,187
They look like leftovers from construction,

9
00:00:15,187 --> 00:00:17,030
but they are one of the most

10
00:00:17,030 --> 00:00:19,661
carefully designed parts of the railway.

11
00:00:19,841 --> 00:00:22,749
Without them, a track would sink,

12
00:00:22,749 --> 00:00:25,569
flood, and twist out of shape in

13
00:00:25,569 --> 00:00:26,714
a few months.

14
00:00:26,894 --> 00:00:30,461
Today, ten parts of a railway track,

15
00:00:30,461 --> 00:00:32,839
and why each one exists.

16
00:00:33,188 --> 00:00:35,731
A railway track looks simple.

17
00:00:35,911 --> 00:00:37,379
Two steel rails,

18
00:00:37,379 --> 00:00:39,488
some blocks under them,

19
00:00:39,488 --> 00:00:40,497
and stones.

20
00:00:40,677 --> 00:00:43,534
But every train that passes hits it

21
00:00:43,534 --> 00:00:45,167
with enormous force,

22
00:00:45,167 --> 00:00:46,473
again and again,

23
00:00:46,473 --> 00:00:48,432
in rain, heat and frost.

24
00:00:48,612 --> 00:00:51,178
A loaded freight car can weigh more

25
00:00:51,178 --> 00:00:52,791
than one hundred tons,

26
00:00:52,791 --> 00:00:55,210
all carried by just eight wheels,

27
00:00:55,210 --> 00:00:57,409
and a train can have more than

28
00:00:57,409 --> 00:00:58,655
one hundred cars.

29
00:00:58,835 --> 00:01:01,468
The track has to take that punishment

30
00:01:01,468 --> 00:01:03,745
every day and stay accurate to a

31
00:01:03,745 --> 00:01:04,884
few millimeters.

32
00:01:05,233 --> 00:01:07,851
As always, every fact comes from a

33
00:01:07,851 --> 00:01:11,314
named source, railway engineering references,

34
00:01:11,314 --> 00:01:14,778
infrastructure managers, and railway history,

35
00:01:14,778 --> 00:01:17,087
all listed in the description.

36
00:01:17,267 --> 00:01:19,206
Let's start with the rocks.

37
00:01:19,556 --> 00:01:22,471
Why are there rocks under railway tracks?

38
00:01:22,651 --> 00:01:24,424
They are called ballast,

39
00:01:24,424 --> 00:01:26,714
and they do three jobs at once.

40
00:01:26,894 --> 00:01:29,442
First, they spread the weight of the

41
00:01:29,442 --> 00:01:31,777
train over the soft ground below,

42
00:01:31,777 --> 00:01:32,838
like snowshoes.

43
00:01:33,018 --> 00:01:35,978
Second, they lock the sleepers in place,

44
00:01:35,978 --> 00:01:38,715
so the track cannot slide sideways or

45
00:01:38,715 --> 00:01:39,973
along its length.

46
00:01:40,153 --> 00:01:42,943
And third, they let rain drain away

47
00:01:42,943 --> 00:01:43,740
instantly.

48
00:01:43,920 --> 00:01:46,452
Water is the enemy of a railway,

49
00:01:46,452 --> 00:01:49,379
because wet ground turns soft and the

50
00:01:49,379 --> 00:01:50,329
track sinks.

51
00:01:50,678 --> 00:01:52,490
The stones are not random.

52
00:01:52,670 --> 00:01:54,494
They are crushed rock,

53
00:01:54,494 --> 00:01:57,976
usually hard stone like granite or basalt,

54
00:01:57,976 --> 00:02:01,210
broken into pieces of a few centimeters

55
00:02:01,210 --> 00:02:02,619
with sharp edges.

56
00:02:02,799 --> 00:02:04,425
Sharp edges matter.

57
00:02:04,605 --> 00:02:07,370
Angular stones lock together like a puzzle

58
00:02:07,370 --> 00:02:08,819
when they are pressed.

59
00:02:08,999 --> 00:02:12,249
Round river pebbles would roll like marbles,

60
00:02:12,249 --> 00:02:14,834
and the track would move every time

61
00:02:14,834 --> 00:02:15,942
a train passed.

62
00:02:16,291 --> 00:02:19,031
Ballast also keeps plants away.

63
00:02:19,211 --> 00:02:22,128
Grass and weeds hold water and fill

64
00:02:22,128 --> 00:02:24,128
the gaps between stones,

65
00:02:24,128 --> 00:02:27,961
so railways regularly send weed control trains

66
00:02:27,961 --> 00:02:29,544
to spray the track.

67
00:02:29,724 --> 00:02:31,304
And the layer is deep,

68
00:02:31,304 --> 00:02:33,672
often around a quarter of a meter

69
00:02:33,672 --> 00:02:35,611
or more under the sleepers.

70
00:02:35,791 --> 00:02:38,918
Those gray rocks are a foundation,

71
00:02:38,918 --> 00:02:40,758
a drain and a clamp,

72
00:02:40,758 --> 00:02:41,770
all in one.

73
00:02:42,119 --> 00:02:44,373
Why are there blocks across the track

74
00:02:44,373 --> 00:02:45,347
under the rails?

75
00:02:45,527 --> 00:02:48,705
They are called sleepers in Britain and

76
00:02:48,705 --> 00:02:50,008
ties in America.

77
00:02:50,188 --> 00:02:52,333
Their job is to hold the two

78
00:02:52,333 --> 00:02:55,550
rails at exactly the right distance apart,

79
00:02:55,550 --> 00:02:56,852
called the gauge,

80
00:02:56,852 --> 00:02:59,073
and to pass the load from the

81
00:02:59,073 --> 00:03:00,835
rails into the ballast.

82
00:03:01,015 --> 00:03:03,110
A main line has well over a

83
00:03:03,110 --> 00:03:05,903
thousand of them in every kilometer.

84
00:03:06,083 --> 00:03:08,351
If the gauge opens even a few

85
00:03:08,351 --> 00:03:11,558
centimeters, a wheel can drop between the

86
00:03:11,558 --> 00:03:12,027
rails.

87
00:03:12,376 --> 00:03:14,534
For more than a century,

88
00:03:14,534 --> 00:03:16,872
sleepers were mostly wood,

89
00:03:16,872 --> 00:03:19,120
often soaked in creosote,

90
00:03:19,120 --> 00:03:23,616
a dark tar-like preservative that protects against

91
00:03:23,616 --> 00:03:25,055
rot and insects.

92
00:03:25,235 --> 00:03:27,552
That smell of a railway on a

93
00:03:27,552 --> 00:03:29,704
hot day is often creosote.

94
00:03:29,884 --> 00:03:33,588
Many modern main lines use concrete sleepers

95
00:03:33,588 --> 00:03:34,261
instead.

96
00:03:34,441 --> 00:03:35,728
They are heavier,

97
00:03:35,728 --> 00:03:37,846
around a quarter of a ton or

98
00:03:37,846 --> 00:03:40,343
more each, so they hold the track

99
00:03:40,343 --> 00:03:42,915
more firmly, and they can last for

100
00:03:42,915 --> 00:03:43,520
decades.

101
00:03:43,870 --> 00:03:45,539
Wood is still everywhere,

102
00:03:45,539 --> 00:03:46,007
though.

103
00:03:46,187 --> 00:03:47,716
In North America,

104
00:03:47,716 --> 00:03:49,964
most ties are still wood,

105
00:03:49,964 --> 00:03:52,482
because wood is easy to cut,

106
00:03:52,482 --> 00:03:54,370
handles impacts well,

107
00:03:54,370 --> 00:03:57,158
and is simple to replace one by

108
00:03:57,158 --> 00:03:57,518
one.

109
00:03:57,698 --> 00:04:01,086
Some railways now use sleepers made of

110
00:04:01,086 --> 00:04:04,652
recycled plastic or composite materials.

111
00:04:04,832 --> 00:04:06,780
The block under the rail is one

112
00:04:06,780 --> 00:04:08,852
of the most replaced parts in the

113
00:04:08,852 --> 00:04:09,732
whole railway.

114
00:04:10,081 --> 00:04:13,044
Why are modern rails held down by

115
00:04:13,044 --> 00:04:16,545
small curled pieces of steel instead of

116
00:04:16,545 --> 00:04:17,442
big nails?

117
00:04:17,622 --> 00:04:19,526
Those clips are springs.

118
00:04:19,706 --> 00:04:21,131
On wooden sleepers,

119
00:04:21,131 --> 00:04:24,132
rails were traditionally held by spikes,

120
00:04:24,132 --> 00:04:25,407
like giant nails.

121
00:04:25,587 --> 00:04:28,879
But spikes slowly loosen as trains shake

122
00:04:28,879 --> 00:04:29,290
them.

123
00:04:29,470 --> 00:04:32,092
A spring clip presses down on the

124
00:04:32,092 --> 00:04:34,237
rail with a constant force,

125
00:04:34,237 --> 00:04:36,939
and if the rail moves or vibrates,

126
00:04:36,939 --> 00:04:39,481
the clip moves with it and keeps

127
00:04:39,481 --> 00:04:40,117
pushing.

128
00:04:40,466 --> 00:04:43,323
One of the most famous designs is

129
00:04:43,323 --> 00:04:44,795
the Pandrol clip,

130
00:04:44,795 --> 00:04:48,344
developed in the nineteen fifties from an

131
00:04:48,344 --> 00:04:50,855
idea by a Norwegian engineer,

132
00:04:50,855 --> 00:04:52,413
Per Pande-Rolfsen.

133
00:04:52,593 --> 00:04:55,252
The name combines parts of his name.

134
00:04:55,432 --> 00:04:57,954
It became one of the most widely

135
00:04:57,954 --> 00:05:00,633
used rail fastenings in the world.

136
00:05:00,813 --> 00:05:03,687
Workers can install and remove these clips

137
00:05:03,687 --> 00:05:06,424
quickly, which matters on a railway that

138
00:05:06,424 --> 00:05:07,860
has millions of them.

139
00:05:08,210 --> 00:05:09,342
Under the rail,

140
00:05:09,342 --> 00:05:11,833
there is often a thin rubber pad.

141
00:05:12,013 --> 00:05:14,946
It cushions the impact of the wheels,

142
00:05:14,946 --> 00:05:17,166
reduces vibration and noise,

143
00:05:17,166 --> 00:05:19,861
and protects the concrete sleeper.

144
00:05:20,041 --> 00:05:22,384
So a modern track is not just

145
00:05:22,384 --> 00:05:23,838
steel on concrete.

146
00:05:24,018 --> 00:05:24,984
It is steel,

147
00:05:24,984 --> 00:05:27,643
a spring, a cushion and concrete,

148
00:05:27,643 --> 00:05:30,382
each one absorbing a little of the

149
00:05:30,382 --> 00:05:33,282
hammering that every train delivers.

150
00:05:33,632 --> 00:05:35,778
Why does a rail have that strange

151
00:05:35,778 --> 00:05:37,208
shape when you cut it?

152
00:05:37,388 --> 00:05:38,967
Look at the end of a rail.

153
00:05:39,147 --> 00:05:41,268
It has a rounded head on top,

154
00:05:41,268 --> 00:05:43,755
a thin vertical web in the middle,

155
00:05:43,755 --> 00:05:45,729
and a wide flat foot at the

156
00:05:45,729 --> 00:05:46,241
bottom.

157
00:05:46,421 --> 00:05:48,151
It is like a steel beam.

158
00:05:48,331 --> 00:05:50,864
The head is thick because the wheels

159
00:05:50,864 --> 00:05:51,779
wear it away.

160
00:05:51,959 --> 00:05:54,392
The tall web makes the rail very

161
00:05:54,392 --> 00:05:56,750
stiff, so it can bridge the gap

162
00:05:56,750 --> 00:05:58,043
between sleepers.

163
00:05:58,223 --> 00:06:00,469
And the wide foot sits flat and

164
00:06:00,469 --> 00:06:02,135
stable on the sleepers.

165
00:06:02,485 --> 00:06:05,837
This flat-bottom design was promoted in Britain

166
00:06:05,837 --> 00:06:08,619
by the engineer Charles Vignoles in the

167
00:06:08,619 --> 00:06:12,470
eighteen thirties, and something similar was developed

168
00:06:12,470 --> 00:06:15,251
in America by Robert Stevens around the

169
00:06:15,251 --> 00:06:15,965
same time.

170
00:06:16,145 --> 00:06:18,732
Earlier rails came in many shapes,

171
00:06:18,732 --> 00:06:20,406
and some broke easily.

172
00:06:20,586 --> 00:06:23,421
The flat-bottom rail won because it puts

173
00:06:23,421 --> 00:06:25,902
the steel exactly where it does the

174
00:06:25,902 --> 00:06:26,611
most work.

175
00:06:26,961 --> 00:06:29,760
Rails are measured by weight per length.

176
00:06:29,940 --> 00:06:32,750
A typical heavy main line rail today

177
00:06:32,750 --> 00:06:35,872
weighs around sixty kilograms per meter.

178
00:06:36,052 --> 00:06:38,142
Every meter of track has two of

179
00:06:38,142 --> 00:06:38,479
them.

180
00:06:38,659 --> 00:06:41,370
Rails are also made of special steel,

181
00:06:41,370 --> 00:06:44,008
hard enough to resist wear but tough

182
00:06:44,008 --> 00:06:45,474
enough not to crack.

183
00:06:45,654 --> 00:06:47,314
They do not last forever.

184
00:06:47,494 --> 00:06:48,547
On busy lines,

185
00:06:48,547 --> 00:06:50,577
the head slowly wears down,

186
00:06:50,577 --> 00:06:53,961
and rails are ground and eventually replaced.

187
00:06:54,310 --> 00:06:57,418
Why do giant yellow machines drive along

188
00:06:57,418 --> 00:06:59,361
tracks shaking the rocks?

189
00:06:59,541 --> 00:07:00,574
They are tampers.

190
00:07:00,754 --> 00:07:03,838
Over time, trains push the ballast around,

191
00:07:03,838 --> 00:07:06,260
and the track sinks a little more

192
00:07:06,260 --> 00:07:08,243
in some places than others.

193
00:07:08,423 --> 00:07:10,699
The tamper measures the track,

194
00:07:10,699 --> 00:07:13,051
lifts it to the correct height,

195
00:07:13,051 --> 00:07:16,389
and then drives vibrating steel fingers into

196
00:07:16,389 --> 00:07:18,590
the ballast on each side of a

197
00:07:18,590 --> 00:07:19,197
sleeper.

198
00:07:19,377 --> 00:07:22,247
They squeeze the stones together under the

199
00:07:22,247 --> 00:07:24,366
sleeper, so it stays at the new

200
00:07:24,366 --> 00:07:24,845
height.

201
00:07:25,194 --> 00:07:27,946
This is the big advantage of ballast.

202
00:07:28,126 --> 00:07:30,936
A track on stones can be adjusted.

203
00:07:31,116 --> 00:07:32,651
Raise it a little here,

204
00:07:32,651 --> 00:07:34,253
shift it a little there,

205
00:07:34,253 --> 00:07:36,189
then pack the stones tightly.

206
00:07:36,369 --> 00:07:39,546
A modern tamping machine uses lasers and

207
00:07:39,546 --> 00:07:42,960
computers, and it can work through hundreds

208
00:07:42,960 --> 00:07:44,786
of sleepers in an hour.

209
00:07:44,966 --> 00:07:47,944
The Austrian company Plasser and Theurer has

210
00:07:47,944 --> 00:07:51,124
been building these machines since the nineteen

211
00:07:51,124 --> 00:07:51,665
fifties.

212
00:07:52,015 --> 00:07:55,405
After tamping, other machines shape the stone

213
00:07:55,405 --> 00:07:57,288
shoulder along the sides,

214
00:07:57,288 --> 00:07:59,925
and some even shake the whole track

215
00:07:59,925 --> 00:08:01,431
to settle it faster.

216
00:08:01,611 --> 00:08:03,328
Then the line can go back to

217
00:08:03,328 --> 00:08:04,003
full speed.

218
00:08:04,183 --> 00:08:06,400
To passengers, it is invisible.

219
00:08:06,580 --> 00:08:08,729
They only notice that the ride is

220
00:08:08,729 --> 00:08:09,576
smooth again.

221
00:08:09,756 --> 00:08:12,453
The comfort of your train trip depends

222
00:08:12,453 --> 00:08:15,363
on stones being squeezed every few years.

223
00:08:15,713 --> 00:08:18,266
Why do some railway machines dig up

224
00:08:18,266 --> 00:08:20,381
all the stones under a track?

225
00:08:20,561 --> 00:08:23,614
Because ballast slowly dies.

226
00:08:23,794 --> 00:08:26,615
Every train crushes the stones a little.

227
00:08:26,795 --> 00:08:28,714
Sharp edges become round,

228
00:08:28,714 --> 00:08:30,632
small pieces become dust,

229
00:08:30,632 --> 00:08:33,164
and dirt from the ground and from

230
00:08:33,164 --> 00:08:34,853
trains fills the gaps.

231
00:08:35,033 --> 00:08:37,865
Railway engineers call this fouling.

232
00:08:38,045 --> 00:08:40,632
Fouled ballast cannot drain water,

233
00:08:40,632 --> 00:08:43,294
and it no longer locks the sleepers

234
00:08:43,294 --> 00:08:43,978
in place.

235
00:08:44,328 --> 00:08:46,975
A ballast cleaner digs the stones out

236
00:08:46,975 --> 00:08:49,408
from under the track with a moving

237
00:08:49,408 --> 00:08:51,840
chain, without removing the rails.

238
00:08:52,020 --> 00:08:54,597
It shakes them through screens,

239
00:08:54,597 --> 00:08:56,425
keeps the good stones,

240
00:08:56,425 --> 00:08:59,583
throws away the dust and small pieces,

241
00:08:59,583 --> 00:09:02,160
and puts the clean stones back,

242
00:09:02,160 --> 00:09:04,071
with fresh stone added.

243
00:09:04,251 --> 00:09:06,362
It is like a giant sieve rolling

244
00:09:06,362 --> 00:09:07,549
along the railway.

245
00:09:07,899 --> 00:09:09,863
If fouling is ignored,

246
00:09:09,863 --> 00:09:13,703
the result is something called mud pumping.

247
00:09:13,883 --> 00:09:15,839
Every time a train passes,

248
00:09:15,839 --> 00:09:18,547
the sleepers push down into wet mud,

249
00:09:18,547 --> 00:09:21,105
and the mud squirts up through the

250
00:09:21,105 --> 00:09:22,835
stones like toothpaste.

251
00:09:23,015 --> 00:09:25,238
You can sometimes see it as gray

252
00:09:25,238 --> 00:09:27,113
stains around the sleepers.

253
00:09:27,293 --> 00:09:29,364
That spot will keep sinking,

254
00:09:29,364 --> 00:09:31,879
so the speed must be reduced until

255
00:09:31,879 --> 00:09:33,284
the track is fixed.

256
00:09:33,634 --> 00:09:36,425
Why do some railway bridges have extra

257
00:09:36,425 --> 00:09:38,556
rails inside the normal ones?

258
00:09:38,736 --> 00:09:40,187
They are guard rails.

259
00:09:40,367 --> 00:09:42,704
They do nothing when the train runs

260
00:09:42,704 --> 00:09:43,305
normally.

261
00:09:43,485 --> 00:09:45,530
But if a wheel derails on the

262
00:09:45,530 --> 00:09:48,139
bridge, the guard rail catches it and

263
00:09:48,139 --> 00:09:50,748
keeps the train running in a straight

264
00:09:50,748 --> 00:09:52,229
line along the track,

265
00:09:52,229 --> 00:09:55,261
instead of veering sideways and falling off

266
00:09:55,261 --> 00:09:57,800
the bridge or hitting the structure.

267
00:09:58,150 --> 00:10:00,565
The same idea is used in other

268
00:10:00,565 --> 00:10:03,787
risky places, like sharp curves and next

269
00:10:03,787 --> 00:10:05,719
to important structures.

270
00:10:05,899 --> 00:10:08,953
On switches, short check rails help guide

271
00:10:08,953 --> 00:10:11,635
the wheels through the gap where two

272
00:10:11,635 --> 00:10:12,529
rails cross.

273
00:10:12,709 --> 00:10:14,844
These extra rails are a backup for

274
00:10:14,844 --> 00:10:17,608
the moment something has already gone wrong.

275
00:10:17,788 --> 00:10:20,675
They turn a derailment into a controlled

276
00:10:20,675 --> 00:10:21,108
slide.

277
00:10:21,459 --> 00:10:24,663
Railways also use the opposite idea.

278
00:10:24,843 --> 00:10:26,138
In some places,

279
00:10:26,138 --> 00:10:30,112
special devices called derails or catch points

280
00:10:30,112 --> 00:10:33,826
are designed to deliberately push a runaway

281
00:10:33,826 --> 00:10:35,553
wagon off the track,

282
00:10:35,553 --> 00:10:36,935
onto the ground,

283
00:10:36,935 --> 00:10:38,317
in a safe place,

284
00:10:38,317 --> 00:10:40,909
before it can roll onto a main

285
00:10:40,909 --> 00:10:41,341
line.

286
00:10:41,521 --> 00:10:44,630
Sometimes the safest thing for a train

287
00:10:44,630 --> 00:10:46,594
is to stay on the rails.

288
00:10:46,774 --> 00:10:49,245
Sometimes it is to leave them on

289
00:10:49,245 --> 00:10:49,862
purpose.

290
00:10:50,213 --> 00:10:52,897
Why are there small boxes bolted to

291
00:10:52,897 --> 00:10:54,891
the side of railway rails?

292
00:10:55,071 --> 00:10:57,637
Many of them are axle counters.

293
00:10:57,817 --> 00:11:00,733
Each one counts the wheels entering and

294
00:11:00,733 --> 00:11:02,751
leaving a section of track.

295
00:11:02,931 --> 00:11:05,315
If the same number goes out as

296
00:11:05,315 --> 00:11:07,936
came in, the section is empty and

297
00:11:07,936 --> 00:11:10,002
the signal can turn green.

298
00:11:10,182 --> 00:11:12,341
If not, it stays red.

299
00:11:12,521 --> 00:11:14,646
Signals do not see trains.

300
00:11:14,826 --> 00:11:15,801
They count them.

301
00:11:16,152 --> 00:11:19,019
The older method is even more clever.

302
00:11:19,199 --> 00:11:21,114
In eighteen seventy-two,

303
00:11:21,114 --> 00:11:24,944
the American William Robinson created the closed

304
00:11:24,944 --> 00:11:26,061
track circuit.

305
00:11:26,241 --> 00:11:29,704
A small electric current runs through the

306
00:11:29,704 --> 00:11:30,211
rails.

307
00:11:30,391 --> 00:11:31,902
When a train enters,

308
00:11:31,902 --> 00:11:34,771
its steel wheels and axles connect the

309
00:11:34,771 --> 00:11:37,188
two rails and short the circuit,

310
00:11:37,188 --> 00:11:39,076
and the signal turns red.

311
00:11:39,256 --> 00:11:41,775
If a wire breaks or the power

312
00:11:41,775 --> 00:11:44,643
fails, the signal also turns red.

313
00:11:44,823 --> 00:11:46,436
The failure is safe.

314
00:11:46,787 --> 00:11:49,979
That is also why railway workers sometimes

315
00:11:49,979 --> 00:11:52,640
carry a simple wire with two clips,

316
00:11:52,640 --> 00:11:55,529
called a track circuit operating clip.

317
00:11:55,709 --> 00:11:57,156
If there is a danger,

318
00:11:57,156 --> 00:11:59,776
clipping it across the two rails makes

319
00:11:59,776 --> 00:12:02,120
the signal behind think there is a

320
00:12:02,120 --> 00:12:03,429
train and turn red.

321
00:12:03,609 --> 00:12:04,896
A piece of wire,

322
00:12:04,896 --> 00:12:08,033
two clips, and the signal protects you.

323
00:12:08,213 --> 00:12:10,872
Simple systems save lives.

324
00:12:11,222 --> 00:12:13,908
Why are there flat yellow boxes lying

325
00:12:13,908 --> 00:12:16,957
between the rails on many modern railways?

326
00:12:17,137 --> 00:12:18,326
They are balises,

327
00:12:18,326 --> 00:12:20,005
and they talk to trains.

328
00:12:20,185 --> 00:12:22,580
When a train passes over one,

329
00:12:22,580 --> 00:12:25,803
an antenna under the train sends energy

330
00:12:25,803 --> 00:12:28,529
down, and the balise wakes up and

331
00:12:28,529 --> 00:12:29,851
sends back data,

332
00:12:29,851 --> 00:12:31,834
like the exact location,

333
00:12:31,834 --> 00:12:33,651
the speed limit ahead,

334
00:12:33,651 --> 00:12:36,543
or the distance to the next signal.

335
00:12:36,723 --> 00:12:38,755
The balise has no battery.

336
00:12:38,935 --> 00:12:41,489
It is powered by the train itself.

337
00:12:41,839 --> 00:12:44,544
In Europe, these are part of the

338
00:12:44,544 --> 00:12:47,080
European Train Control System,

339
00:12:47,080 --> 00:12:51,054
which is replacing dozens of different national

340
00:12:51,054 --> 00:12:51,730
systems.

341
00:12:51,910 --> 00:12:54,390
The computer on the train uses the

342
00:12:54,390 --> 00:12:57,161
information to calculate a safe speed,

343
00:12:57,161 --> 00:12:59,495
and if the driver goes too fast,

344
00:12:59,495 --> 00:13:01,756
the train brakes automatically.

345
00:13:01,936 --> 00:13:04,432
The train does not just obey signals.

346
00:13:04,612 --> 00:13:06,615
It knows the rules of the track

347
00:13:06,615 --> 00:13:07,003
ahead.

348
00:13:07,354 --> 00:13:10,527
Balises also tell the train exactly where

349
00:13:10,527 --> 00:13:13,313
it is, which is surprisingly hard on

350
00:13:13,313 --> 00:13:14,087
a railway.

351
00:13:14,267 --> 00:13:16,140
Wheels slip and slide,

352
00:13:16,140 --> 00:13:19,546
so counting turns gives errors over long

353
00:13:19,546 --> 00:13:20,397
distances.

354
00:13:20,577 --> 00:13:22,853
A balise is a fixed point that

355
00:13:22,853 --> 00:13:24,978
resets the train's position.

356
00:13:25,158 --> 00:13:26,088
In a tunnel,

357
00:13:26,088 --> 00:13:28,104
without satellite signals,

358
00:13:28,104 --> 00:13:30,663
a simple yellow box on the ground

359
00:13:30,663 --> 00:13:33,066
can be the most reliable map in

360
00:13:33,066 --> 00:13:33,842
the world.

361
00:13:34,192 --> 00:13:36,844
Why do some railways have sensors beside

362
00:13:36,844 --> 00:13:39,033
the track pointing at the wheels?

363
00:13:39,213 --> 00:13:41,465
They are hot box detectors.

364
00:13:41,645 --> 00:13:43,007
As a train passes,

365
00:13:43,007 --> 00:13:46,716
infrared sensors measure the temperature of every

366
00:13:46,716 --> 00:13:47,775
wheel bearing.

367
00:13:47,955 --> 00:13:50,827
A bearing that is failing gets very

368
00:13:50,827 --> 00:13:53,124
hot, and if nothing is done,

369
00:13:53,124 --> 00:13:54,191
it can seize,

370
00:13:54,191 --> 00:13:55,749
the axle can break,

371
00:13:55,749 --> 00:13:57,801
and the train can derail.

372
00:13:57,981 --> 00:14:01,003
The detector finds it minutes or hours

373
00:14:01,003 --> 00:14:02,276
before it fails.

374
00:14:02,627 --> 00:14:05,232
On many North American railways,

375
00:14:05,232 --> 00:14:08,082
these detectors talk to the crew by

376
00:14:08,082 --> 00:14:08,571
radio.

377
00:14:08,751 --> 00:14:10,725
After the train passes,

378
00:14:10,725 --> 00:14:13,900
a robotic voice announces the result,

379
00:14:13,900 --> 00:14:16,646
often something like no defects.

380
00:14:16,826 --> 00:14:18,488
If there is a problem,

381
00:14:18,488 --> 00:14:20,680
it tells the crew which axle,

382
00:14:20,680 --> 00:14:22,418
counted from the front,

383
00:14:22,418 --> 00:14:24,837
so they can stop and inspect it.

384
00:14:25,017 --> 00:14:28,185
Other detectors along the line check for

385
00:14:28,185 --> 00:14:30,641
dragging equipment, high loads,

386
00:14:30,641 --> 00:14:32,621
and flat spots on wheels.

387
00:14:32,971 --> 00:14:35,877
The name comes from the old days,

388
00:14:35,877 --> 00:14:38,783
when axles ran in boxes of grease

389
00:14:38,783 --> 00:14:41,248
or oil called journal boxes.

390
00:14:41,428 --> 00:14:43,865
If the lubrication failed,

391
00:14:43,865 --> 00:14:45,645
the box overheated,

392
00:14:45,645 --> 00:14:49,393
sometimes smoking or even catching fire.

393
00:14:49,573 --> 00:14:52,789
Railway workers called it a hot box.

394
00:14:52,969 --> 00:14:54,811
The technology changed,

395
00:14:54,811 --> 00:14:56,974
but the danger is the same.

396
00:14:57,154 --> 00:14:59,909
A tiny bearing can stop a train

397
00:14:59,909 --> 00:15:01,775
of ten thousand tons.

398
00:15:02,125 --> 00:15:03,622
So here are the ten.

399
00:15:03,802 --> 00:15:06,003
Sharp rocks that spread,

400
00:15:06,003 --> 00:15:07,378
lock and drain.

401
00:15:07,558 --> 00:15:09,826
Sleepers that hold the gauge,

402
00:15:09,826 --> 00:15:12,562
and spring clips that never let go.

403
00:15:12,742 --> 00:15:14,809
A rail shaped like a beam.

404
00:15:14,989 --> 00:15:17,745
A machine that squeezes the stones,

405
00:15:17,745 --> 00:15:19,714
and one that washes them.

406
00:15:19,894 --> 00:15:21,914
Extra rails on bridges,

407
00:15:21,914 --> 00:15:23,934
boxes that count axles,

408
00:15:23,934 --> 00:15:26,833
yellow boxes that talk to trains,

409
00:15:26,833 --> 00:15:30,171
and sensors that check every wheel for

410
00:15:30,171 --> 00:15:30,610
heat.

411
00:15:30,960 --> 00:15:32,863
Next time you see rocks under a

412
00:15:32,863 --> 00:15:35,134
railway, you will know they are doing

413
00:15:35,134 --> 00:15:36,300
three jobs at once.

414
00:15:36,480 --> 00:15:39,409
If you want more machines explained part

415
00:15:39,409 --> 00:15:42,192
by part, subscribe to Machine Reverse.

416
00:15:42,372 --> 00:15:44,455
And tell us in the comments which

417
00:15:44,455 --> 00:15:46,538
machine you want us to take apart

418
00:15:46,538 --> 00:15:46,854
next.
